<!--
    sharedpush: one push above a branch is the argument of the call on each arm.
    pkgen2.exe 0x40621e
      push 0x14; jz L; call [malloc]; jmp M; L: call _emalloc; M: pop ecx
    printed `malloc(0x14)` and `_emalloc()`, although both callees receive 0x14.
    The upstream double-use test refuses an input trial whose value another call
    reads unless that call's own trial is still open, and a call through a
    declared import slot has no trial, so the declared call always kept the push.
    Stage / sub-stage: P4 call-site input recovery (`check_call_double_use`,
    `p4_calls/kuna_sharedpush.rs`). A strict fix, always on.
    The fixture `sharedpush_pe_i386.exe` is assembled from
    `sharedpush_pe_i386.asm` (see the fixtures README).
    0x401000 pushes 0x14 for malloc or the internal wrapper at 0x4010d0, then the
    result for free or the wrapper at 0x4010e0: every call takes its push
    (asserts 1-4). 0x401040 pushes i in a loop for toupper through the import
    table or tolower through a register; the arms rejoin and loop back through the
    push, and both take i (5-6). 0x401080 runs Note after malloc on one path, on
    the slot malloc took, so Note gains nothing (7-8). 0x4010a0 runs a
    control-flow guard check (target in ECX) before the guarded call on the slot
    that free takes on the other arm; two calls read it on that arm, so neither
    gains it and the guard check is never handed the argument (9-10). 0x401100
    pushes 3 above the shared 0x14 for Note, so 0x14 sits one slot deeper in
    Note's area than it does in malloc's and is not Note's argument (11).
    0x401120 is `if (sel) free(p); else pair(guard_nop(), p);` with p pushed
    once for free and for pair's second argument: guard_nop runs on the slot
    pair then reads, so it is not the only reader on its arm and gains nothing
    (12).
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
